{
  "name": "Content/Case Study Tile",
  "slug": "content-case-study-tile",
  "domain": "components",
  "source": {
    "kind": "code-only",
    "why": "No Figma set. Its contract depends on required props and CSS state, neither of which a Figma variant can express.",
    "component": "src/components/CaseStudyTile.tsx",
    "styles": "src/components/CaseStudyTile.css"
  },
  "description": "The link is on the title, and a stretched pseudo element makes the whole tile\nclickable.\n\nThe obvious approach, wrapping everything in one anchor, gives the link an\naccessible name of the discipline plus the title plus the whole summary,\nwhich is what a screen reader then reads out in a list of links. It also\nmakes it impossible to put any other link inside. This way the accessible\nname is the title alone and the click target is still the whole tile.\n\nThe cue is aria-hidden for the same reason: it is a visual signal that the\ntile is clickable, and repeating it to a screen reader adds nothing to a link\nthat already says what it is.",
  "donts": [
    "Do not add a read more link. The whole tile is already the link, and a second affordance for the same intent splits the click.",
    "Do not put the discipline label on as an eyebrow in uppercase tracked type. It is metadata a reader is scanning for, not a decorative category heading.",
    "Do not write a summary that restates the title. The line has one job: say what the problem was, well enough to earn the click."
  ],
  "props": {
    "title": {
      "type": "string",
      "required": true
    },
    "summary": {
      "type": "string",
      "required": true,
      "note": "One line that says what the problem was. It has to earn the click."
    },
    "discipline": {
      "type": "string",
      "required": true,
      "note": "The discipline this study argues for. Two studies, two disciplines."
    },
    "href": {
      "type": "string",
      "required": true
    },
    "variant": {
      "type": "'bare' | 'card'",
      "required": false,
      "note": "`bare` groups by image and spacing, the way revolut.com does. `card` puts it on a surface with a border. Cards are the lazy grouping: the band already separates this section, and with no shadows available in site chrome a card cannot do the one thing cards are for. Kept as an option because the affordance question is real."
    },
    "image": {
      "type": "{ src?: string; srcSet?: string; sizes?: string; alt: string; ratio?: MediaRatio }",
      "required": false,
      "note": "Plain data rather than a ReactNode slot: JSX written inside an `.astro` expression produces an Astro template object, not a React element."
    }
  },
